 **Revisit if:** something is missing for good after a first run in a game: a pass drawn once into a steady target.
-## D115 — One indexed shader cache file, prepared before the game draws (2026-11-07, Mad Riders)
+## D127 — One indexed shader cache file, prepared before the game draws (2026-11-07, Mad Riders)
 
 **Decision:** the pipeline storage (D42) and the driver's pipeline cache (D45) become one file, by default
 `<cache root>/native_x_renderer/<title id>.cache`, or where the game puts it with `SetShaderCache` (Mad Riders:
@@ -4364,14 +4364,14 @@ so the shipped microcode cannot be translated ahead; their variants are kept as
 translations are guesses (no MSAA centroids, no PsParamGen); what draws ask that was not guessed is translated as
 before and kept.
 
-## D116 — Scene draws may wait for a first-time compile, within a budget per frame (2026-11-07, Mad Riders)
+## D128 — Scene draws may wait for a first-time compile, within a budget per frame (2026-11-07, Mad Riders)
 
 **Decision:** `native_x_renderer_scene_wait_ms` (default 0, as before): the draws that D101 does not make wait (the
 scene's) also wait for their shaders' translation and their pipeline, as long as the frame's total wait stays within
 that many milliseconds; past it they are skipped (D28, D34) until the compile is done. The offscreen waits of D101
 are unchanged (and not counted). The log's line counts the scene draws that waited and how long.
 
-**Why:** Mad Riders, first run with the shader cache (D115) prepared: what is left to compile while racing is the
+**Why:** Mad Riders, first run with the shader cache (D127) prepared: what is left to compile while racing is the
 vertex shaders' variants for each vertex layout (the game's D3D patches their fetches, so they cannot be compiled
 ahead) and their pipelines, about 70-130 skipped draws per 10 s on a first lap: objects blinking out and back. The
 offscreen draws that already wait took 5.6 ms on average: most compiles fit a few milliseconds of a frame. Waiting
@@ -4380,16 +4380,16 @@ without a budget (D101 on every draw) made each new pipeline a visible stall (Ma
 **Revisit if:** a game's compiles are mostly longer than a frame: the budget then only delays the frame without
 saving the draw.
 
-## D117 — Compressed cache records, a burst wait budget, more pipeline threads (2026-11-07, Mad Riders)
+## D129 — Compressed cache records, a burst wait budget, more pipeline threads (2026-11-07, Mad Riders)
 
 **Decision:** (1) translations' SPIR-V and the driver's cache are stored compressed in the shader cache file (`src/lz.h`:
 an LZ77 block format laid out as LZ4's; a blob starting `NXZ1`, so files with uncompressed SPIR-V still read). They
 are compressed by whoever stores them (a worker, the save thread) and decompressed on the worker that makes the host
 shader, or on the preparing thread for the driver's cache: never on the renderer's thread. (2) A frame in which at
-least 8 draws were skipped for compiles lets the next frame's scene draws wait up to 33 ms instead of the D116 budget
+least 8 draws were skipped for compiles lets the next frame's scene draws wait up to 33 ms instead of the D128 budget
 (only when the budget is on). (3) DiligentCore compiles pipelines on cores − 2 threads (4 to 12) instead of 4.
 
-**Why:** Mad Riders after D116: the file was 73 MB after the first launch, 93 MB after a race; the race's start still
+**Why:** Mad Riders after D128: the file was 73 MB after the first launch, 93 MB after a race; the race's start still
 skipped about 2,000 draws for compiles (about 1,460 for pipelines) while lapping skipped 2–22 per 600 frames. A
 burst comes when the frames are slow anyway (the countdown, a menu's first visit), so waiting longer there costs
 little and saves most of those draws; more threads finish the burst sooner.
